TIME(1)                        P32W Unix                         TIME(1)

NAME
       time - time a command

SYNOPSIS
       time [-l] command [arg ...]

DESCRIPTION
       time runs the command with its arguments, searching PATH for
       it as the shell does, waits for it, and then prints on the
       standard error, after a blank line, the elapsed real time,
       the user CPU time and the system CPU time it took, in
       seconds:

              real      3.42
              user      2.80
              sys       0.36

       The clock ticks 50 times a second, so the times are good to
       0.02 s. The CPU times include those of all the command's own
       children that it waited for.

       While the command runs, time ignores interrupt and quit, so
       ^C stops the command but the times are still printed.

OPTIONS
       -l     also print the command's memory events (as BSD's
              time -l), counted by the kernel for it and its
              children:

                     26  TLB refills
                      0  copy-on-write page copies
                      0  stack pages added

              A TLB refill happens when the program touches a page
              the TLB (64 entries for instructions, 64 for data)
              does not hold; each costs about 25 instructions. A
              copy-on-write copy is a page shared after fork(2)
              that one side wrote to.

EXAMPLES
       time cc -o hello hello.c
       time -l sort big.txt > /dev/null
       time sort big.txt > /dev/null

EXIT STATUS
       The command's exit status; 128 plus the signal number if it
       was killed by a signal; 127 if it could not be run; 2 for a
       usage error or if time could not fork.

SEE ALSO
       sh(1), top(1), times(2), wait(2)

BUGS
       time is a separate program, not part of the shell, so it
       cannot time a pipeline or a shell built-in; use
       time sh -c 'pipeline'.
